    <body>
      <sec id="article-23704.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy is the most common liver disorder unique to pregnancy, typically presenting in the late second or third trimester with new-onset pruritus, often involving the palms and soles, and elevated serum bile acids due to hepatic accumulation. Hormonal and inflammatory mechanisms contribute to its pathophysiology. Maternal complications are generally limited to pruritus, although preeclampsia risk is increased, while fetal and neonatal complications include preterm birth, respiratory distress syndrome, birth asphyxia, meconium-stained amniotic fluid, and stillbirth. This course equips clinicians with knowledge to recognize clinical features, interpret serum bile acids and liver function tests, and perform risk assessment and counseling. Evidence-based management strategies, including first-line treatment with ursodeoxycholic acid and monitoring considerations, are reviewed. Emphasis on interprofessional collaboration highlights how coordinated care among obstetrics, hepatology, nursing, and pharmacy enhances maternal and fetal outcomes, promotes timely interventions, and supports optimal patient-centered care.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the clinical features associated with intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Screen pregnant individuals presenting with pruritus without rash for intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy using evidence-based diagnostic thresholds.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply current evidence-based management strategies for intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement interprofessional collaboration and communication strategies to manage individuals with intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy, enhancing care coordination and patient outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=23704&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=23704">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23704.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy (ICP) is the most common liver disease specific to pregnancy. ICP is an inflammatory disease of multifactorial etiology; the disease typically presents with new-onset pruritus, typically in the second and third trimesters, and elevated bile acids. The effects of ICP are secondary to the accumulation of bile acids within certain tissues, which leads to pruritus.</p>
        <p>The negative fetal effects are thought to be due to the accumulation of the bile acids within the placenta. The fetal and neonatal risks include an increased risk of stillbirth as well as an increased risk of preterm birth, birth asphyxia, respiratory distress syndrome, and meconium-stained fluid. Maternal risks include pruritus as well as an increased risk of the development of&#x000a0;preeclampsia.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23704.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Several factors appear to contribute to the development of ICP. Although the pathogenesis is not entirely clear, it is likely multifactorial. Estrogen and progesterone are suspected of inhibiting bile acid flow in the liver, thereby reducing bile acid metabolism.&#x000a0;Estrogen and progesterone&#x000a0;are naturally higher in the late second to third trimesters, which is thought to explain why ICP does not present until this time in pregnancy.&#x000a0;Additionally, bile acid stasis in the liver leads to inflammation and increased release of proinflammatory markers, including cytokines, which&#x000a0;may explain why preeclampsia is more common in pregnant patients with ICP.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Research also suggests that patients with ICP have differences in the expression of a wide range of genes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r2">[2]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23704.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Reported prevalence rates vary across the world. The reported incidence rates range from 0.3% to 15%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Most available estimates will quote an incidence rate of 0.3% to 0.5%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;ICP prevalence rates may also vary by ethnicity. In a&#x000a0;Hispanic population in California, the prevalence of ICP was 5.6%. This contrasts with a prevalence rate of 0.32% in a mostly White patient population in Connecticut.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>ICP is a disease unique to pregnancy. While all pregnant individuals are therefore at risk, there are specific factors that can increase the risk of the development of ICP. These factors include a history of hepatobiliary disease outside of pregnancy, a history of ICP in a prior pregnancy, multiple gestations, and advanced maternal age.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23704.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The pathophysiology of pregnancy morbidity in ICP is poorly understood. Fetal death may be related to the sudden development of fetal arrhythmias or vasospasm of chorionic surface vessels of the placenta, which occur due to the elevated bile acid levels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;The increase in preterm labor and spontaneous preterm birth may be related to the fact that bile acids increase the expression of oxytocin receptors in the uterine myometrium.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r10">[10]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23704.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>A liver biopsy is rarely needed in suspected cases of ICP. However, if one is obtained, the histopathology is characterized by cholestasis with bile plugs visible in the hepatocytes and canaliculi.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r11">[11]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23704.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Patients often present with sudden onset intense pruritus. Pruritis can affect any part of the body but often patients present with palmar and plantar itching. Classically, it is not accompanied by a rash, however severe scratching can result in excoriations. Patients may also report that symptoms are worse at night. ICP typically occurs in the late second to early third trimester, although in rare circumstances, it is diagnosed earlier in pregnancy.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23704.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>For patients with new-onset palmar and/or plantar pruritus without a rash, serum total bile acids (TBA) should be drawn. Patients do not need to fast, as there is only a small difference between fasting and postprandial TBA levels. There is no widely used pregnancy-specific normal TBA range.</p>
        <p>Most institutions will use standard laboratory values. In general, it is recommended to use a TBA level greater than 10 &#x003bc;mol/L as a diagnostic criterion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Pruritus associated with ICP may precede the elevation of bile acids by several weeks.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Therefore, TBA should be repeated in 1 to 2 weeks if it is initially normal in a patient with persistent pruritus. Note that if treatment with ursodeoxycholic acid is started to relieve the patient's pruritus, repeat bile acid levels may not increase.&#x000a0;Liver function tests (LFTs) should also be collected during the initial diagnostic workup. ICP can present with mildly to moderately elevated LFT.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Elevation of LFTs may precede the development of elevated bile acids.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23704.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA) is an oral, nonsynthetic bile acid agent used to treat gallstones, but has off-label use in the management of ICP. UDCA is considered the first-line therapy for the treatment of maternal symptoms of ICP as it reduces intrahepatic bile acids as well as decreases their reabsorption in the bowel. This agent has clearer maternal than fetal benefits and improves maternal pruritus within 1 to 2 weeks of treatment initiation. Data do not support the use of UDCA to prevent adverse perinatal outcomes, as there is no evidence that UDCA reduces total bile acid levels. There is evidence that UDCA can reduce alanine transferase (ALT) levels, improving maternal transaminitis, but this does not affect fetal or neonatal outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>&#x000a0;<bold>UDCA</bold></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Dosing: Initiate UDCA at 10&#x02013;15 mg/kg/day.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Can uptitrate to a maximum dose of 20 mg/kg/day divided into&#x000a0;2 to 3 daily doses.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>In typical prescribing, patients are started on 300 mg of UDCA daily, with uptitration to a maximum of 300 mg&#x000a0;3 times daily.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Patient instructions: Take medication with milk or food.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Adverse&#x000a0;effects: Nausea and dizziness.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fetal effects: No human studies exist, but in animals receiving doses higher than those in humans, no adverse fetal effects have been observed.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Obstetric Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>The management of ICP must be an interprofessional team approach. Typically, whenever feasible, an interprofessional team should include an obstetric&#x000a0;clinician,&#x000a0;a maternal-fetal medicine subspecialist, and a hepatologist.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Antenatal Surveillance and Delivery Timing</bold>
</p>
        <p>Different hypotheses have been proposed as to why stillbirth occurs in pregnancies complicated by ICP (see <bold>Table 1</bold>). There is uncertainty whether stillbirth in the setting of ICP results from placental dysfunction or if it is related to bile acid accumulation in the fetal cardiac muscle that results in sudden death. Regardless, antenatal surveillance is recommended given the increased risk of stillbirth with ICP.</p>
        <p>The Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine and the American College of Obstetrics and Gynecology recommend initiation of antepartum testing at the time of diagnosis, as long as a gestational age has been reached at which intervention in the form of delivery would be acceptable for abnormal fetal testing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Antepartum testing can consist of once- or twice-weekly non-stress testing and measurement of the deepest vertical pocket of amniotic fluid. ICP is not typically associated with fetal growth restriction, but the decision to perform serial growth ultrasounds can be left to the discretion of the obstetric&#x000a0;clinician. If delivery is recommended before 37 0/7 weeks, consideration should be given to administering&#x000a0;antenatal corticosteroids.&#x000a0;</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-23704.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
