      <sec id="article-23704.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Maternal prognosis in intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy (ICP) is generally benign, with pruritus and abnormal liver biochemistry resolving rapidly after delivery. However, in some women, abnormalities on liver function tests may persist and warrant further evaluation to exclude alternative etiologies. Large cohort studies have shown that women with ICP have a higher long-term incidence of hepatobiliary disorders, including hepatitis C, chronic hepatitis, hepatic fibrosis or cirrhosis, gallstones, and cholangitis, compared with the general population.</p>
        <p>Fetal prognosis is discussed in the complications section. Additionally, children born to women with ICP have been found to have an increased risk of elevated body mass index and dyslipidemia by age 16.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r18">[18]</xref> The recurrence rate of ICP in subsequent pregnancies is high, occurring in approximately 60% to 70% of cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r19">[19]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23704.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>ICP is associated with increased risk of PTL, premature rupture of membranes (PPROM), meconium-stained fluid, admission to the neonatal intensive care unit, and stillbirth. The risk of fetal death is increased in ICP, with the highest correlation in patients with bile acid levels &#x02265;100 &#x000b5;mol/L.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The sequelae of ICP are related to bile acid accumulation in muscle tissue. Bile acids activate oxytocin receptors, and this likely leads to PTL.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;In the fetus, colonic stimulation can result in meconium-stained fluid at birth. Stillbirth may result from bile acid buildup in the cardiac muscle, resulting in arrhythmias and sudden cardiac arrest, although the exact mechanism is unclear.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>ICP does not carry a significant risk to the pregnant individual. However, as previously discussed, pruritus can cause significant discomfort to the pregnant patient. Additionally, patients with ICP are more likely to develop preeclampsia. Several mechanisms have been suggested, including the accumulation of bile acids in the placenta or altered renal function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;As an association, the risks associated with preeclampsia can further complicate pregnancies affected by ICP.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23704.s13" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>General obstetricians should consider outpatient referral to MFM subspecialists for patients diagnosed with ICP in the first or second trimester, or for those with bile acids greater than 40 &#x000b5;mol/L. Patients who were diagnosed with ICP in the first or early second trimester should be referred to hepatology, as they may have an underlying hepatic diagnosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;Additionally, referral to a hepatologist should be considered, particularly if there is no access to an MFM subspecialist, for patients with ICP diagnosed before the third trimester and for those with severely elevated bile acids (&#x0003e;100 &#x000b5;mol/L).&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23704.s14" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Patient education and deterrence in ICP should focus on early recognition, symptom reporting, and appropriate monitoring to reduce the risk of adverse outcomes. Patients should be counseled to promptly report new-onset pruritus, particularly involving the palms and soles, as early evaluation and measurement of serum bile acids and liver function tests are essential for diagnosis and risk stratification. Education should include the importance of adherence to prescribed treatment, such as UDCA, attending regular prenatal visits, and complying with recommended fetal surveillance and delivery planning to reduce fetal risk.</p>
        <p>Patient education should emphasize that pruritus typically resolves within days after delivery, and serum bile acids and liver function tests usually normalize within a few weeks postpartum.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r22">[22]</xref> Routine repeat laboratory testing during pregnancy is not recommended; however, if laboratory abnormalities persist after delivery, referral to hepatology is necessary to evaluate for other hepatobiliary disorders. Patients should also be counseled that ICP has a high recurrence risk of 60% to 70% in future pregnancies, and currently, there are no established preventive measures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-23704.r23">[23]</xref> Providing reassurance, setting expectations for postpartum resolution, and emphasizing the importance of follow-up care are essential components of patient counseling.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23704.s15" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Following diagnosis of ICP, if TBA are less than 40 &#x000b5;mol/L, it would be reasonable to repeat total bile acid testing around 34&#x000a0;to 36 weeks' gestation to determine delivery timing.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23704.s16" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>ICP&#x000a0;represents the most common liver disease specific to pregnancy and typically presents in the late second to third trimester with new-onset pruritus and elevated serum bile acids. The condition arises from multifactorial mechanisms involving hormonal influences, bile acid stasis, inflammation, and genetic susceptibility. Accumulation of bile acids contributes to significant maternal discomfort and is associated with increased fetal risks, including preterm birth, meconium-stained fluid, respiratory distress, and stillbirth, particularly at higher bile acid levels. Maternal risks are generally limited but include an increased likelihood of preeclampsia.</p>
        <p>Optimal management of ICP requires coordinated interprofessional care. Physicians, general practitioners, and advanced practitioners must promptly recognize symptoms, order and interpret bile acid and liver function tests, initiate evidence-based treatment, and assess maternal and fetal risk. Nurses play a key role in symptom assessment, patient education, and monitoring, while pharmacists support safe dosing and counseling for UDCA. Effective communication among obstetric, laboratory, and neonatal teams supports timely surveillance, shared decision-making, and care coordination, enhancing patient-centered care, safety, and overall team performance.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-23704.s17">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
